1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,442
An idea for an AI feature arrives constantly, from a customer request, a

2
00:00:04,442 --> 00:00:07,227
competitor's launch, an executive who saw a demo.

3
00:00:07,227 --> 00:00:09,557
Most of them sound plausible in the room.

4
00:00:09,557 --> 00:00:14,217
A discovery sprint is how you find out which ones actually hold up, in days, using

5
00:00:14,217 --> 00:00:18,820
tools you already have, rather than months of engineering time discovering it the

6
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:19,616
expensive way.

7
00:00:19,616 --> 00:00:21,321
The sprint isn't new material.

8
00:00:21,321 --> 00:00:25,924
It's five questions this course already taught you to answer, asked in a specific

9
00:00:25,924 --> 00:00:28,766
order, against a real idea, under a real deadline.

10
00:00:28,766 --> 00:00:33,369
That order matters as much as the questions themselves, because each day's answer

11
00:00:33,369 --> 00:00:36,040
decides whether the next day is worth spending.

12
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:40,701
None of the five days ask you to do anything you haven't already practised in this

13
00:00:40,701 --> 00:00:41,099
course.

14
00:00:41,099 --> 00:00:42,121
That's deliberate.

15
00:00:42,121 --> 00:00:46,668
A sprint built from unfamiliar techniques takes longer to run than the five days

16
00:00:46,668 --> 00:00:48,259
it's supposed to fit inside.

17
00:00:48,259 --> 00:00:52,920
A sprint built entirely from tools you've already used once, for real, runs at the

18
00:00:52,920 --> 00:00:54,909
speed the format actually promises.

19
00:00:55,459 --> 00:00:59,554
Read the sequence as a series of exits, not a fixed five-day commitment.

20
00:00:59,554 --> 00:01:04,276
Day one is just 1.6's seven archetypes, asked of a real idea: does AI even fit this

21
00:01:04,276 --> 00:01:04,731
problem.

22
00:01:04,731 --> 00:01:09,509
An idea that fails there, on an archetype mismatch, doesn't get four more days spent

23
00:01:09,509 --> 00:01:11,842
confirming what day one already answered.

24
00:01:11,842 --> 00:01:15,596
Most ideas that don't survive a sprint don't survive past day two.

25
00:01:15,596 --> 00:01:20,033
Day three is usually where a genuinely good-sounding idea meets its first real

26
00:01:20,033 --> 00:01:20,659
resistance.

27
00:01:20,659 --> 00:01:25,380
It's 4.2's coverage test, asked under a deadline: if nobody can sketch even a rough

28
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:29,817
golden set, fifty cases you could plausibly score, that's not a paperwork gap.

29
00:01:29,817 --> 00:01:34,539
It's a sign nobody's agreed what "correct" means for this feature yet, and building

30
00:01:34,539 --> 00:01:38,293
anything before that's settled is building on an undefined target.

31
00:01:38,293 --> 00:01:41,991
Push day three specifically on the hard cases, not the easy ones.

32
00:01:41,991 --> 00:01:46,769
An idea that only produces confident golden-set cases for the obvious, common inputs

33
00:01:46,769 --> 00:01:51,377
and goes quiet on the genuinely ambiguous ones is telling you something real: the

34
00:01:51,377 --> 00:01:55,984
feature sounds clear in the room and gets fuzzy the moment someone tries to write

35
00:01:55,984 --> 00:01:57,577
down what counts as correct.

36
00:01:57,577 --> 00:02:02,242
That fuzziness on day three is exactly what would have shown up as a disputed pass

37
00:02:02,242 --> 00:02:05,825
rate months later, just discovered far earlier and far cheaper.

38
00:02:06,375 --> 00:02:10,928
Day four doesn't need 7.2's full model, built properly, with real token counts.

39
00:02:10,928 --> 00:02:15,481
It needs the rough version: an honest order-of-magnitude guess at cost per use,

40
00:02:15,481 --> 00:02:17,902
multiplied by a realistic volume estimate.

41
00:02:17,902 --> 00:02:22,512
That back-of-envelope number has killed more bad ideas by Friday than a polished

42
00:02:22,512 --> 00:02:27,181
cost model ever will, because a rough number that's clearly too high doesn't need

43
00:02:27,181 --> 00:02:29,083
more precision to make its point.

44
00:02:29,083 --> 00:02:33,751
Day two asks the blast radius question a full section later than 6.6 first taught

45
00:02:33,751 --> 00:02:34,615
it, on purpose.

46
00:02:34,615 --> 00:02:37,036
Why wait until day two instead of day one?

47
00:02:37,036 --> 00:02:41,646
Because an idea that survives day one but carries a genuinely high blast radius,

48
00:02:41,646 --> 00:02:45,508
hard to undo, expensive if wrong, isn't automatically disqualified.

49
00:02:45,508 --> 00:02:49,888
It just needs day two's answer in writing before day three's effort is worth

50
00:02:49,888 --> 00:02:54,441
spending, the same discipline 8.1's register asks of any risk with real stakes.

51
00:02:54,441 --> 00:02:59,051
Day five isn't a fresh judgement call, it's an honest summary of the first four.

52
00:02:59,051 --> 00:03:01,011
Go, if all four questions cleared.

53
00:03:01,011 --> 00:03:04,872
No, if any one of them didn't, stated plainly rather than softened.

54
00:03:04,872 --> 00:03:09,713
Or a narrower version, the option most sprints under-use: the same idea, scoped down

55
00:03:09,713 --> 00:03:14,093
to the slice that actually clears every question, instead of either the full

56
00:03:14,093 --> 00:03:15,995
original pitch or nothing at all.

57
00:03:16,545 --> 00:03:20,105
Notice the good column doesn't celebrate saying no more often.

58
00:03:20,105 --> 00:03:24,928
It celebrates a process capable of saying no at all, quickly, when the idea actually

59
00:03:24,928 --> 00:03:25,617
deserves it.

60
00:03:25,617 --> 00:03:29,922
A sprint that always ends in yes isn't evaluating anything, it's performing

61
00:03:29,922 --> 00:03:34,343
evaluation on top of a decision that was already made before day one started.

62
00:03:34,343 --> 00:03:39,051
That's expensive theatre, not discovery, and everyone on the team usually knows it

63
00:03:39,051 --> 00:03:43,701
within a sprint or two, which quietly kills trust in every future sprint's yes as

64
00:03:43,701 --> 00:03:43,988
well.

65
00:03:43,988 --> 00:03:48,524
This connects directly to this lecture's opening claim: a sprint's entire value

66
00:03:48,524 --> 00:03:51,395
depends on it being genuinely capable of stopping.

67
00:03:51,395 --> 00:03:56,103
Remove that capability, deliberately or through quiet organisational pressure, and

68
00:03:56,103 --> 00:03:58,055
you haven't shortened the process.

69
00:03:58,055 --> 00:04:02,016
You've renamed a five-day rubber stamp and kept calling it discovery.

70
00:04:02,566 --> 00:04:07,331
Say the honest caveat plainly, because it's easy to experience a "no" result as the

71
00:04:07,331 --> 00:04:11,177
sprint having wasted a week, and that framing is exactly backwards.

72
00:04:11,177 --> 00:04:15,424
Compare the cost of a week-long sprint against the cost of three months of

73
00:04:15,424 --> 00:04:20,189
engineering discovering the same no, after a launch date's already been promised to

74
00:04:20,189 --> 00:04:20,648
someone.

75
00:04:20,648 --> 00:04:25,126
The sprint that produces a confident no on day two is one of the cheapest wins

76
00:04:25,126 --> 00:04:29,948
available to a team, and it should be reported that way, not quietly filed away as a

77
00:04:29,948 --> 00:04:34,540
project that "didn't work out." Track this the same way 9.1 asked you to track a

78
00:04:34,540 --> 00:04:39,132
real metric: count sprints run against sprints that ended in yes, and watch that

79
00:04:39,132 --> 00:04:40,051
ratio over time.

80
00:04:40,051 --> 00:04:44,643
A ratio sitting at or near one hundred percent yes is the single clearest sign a

81
00:04:44,643 --> 00:04:49,465
sprint has stopped doing its actual job, whatever the individual sprints looked like

82
00:04:49,465 --> 00:04:50,383
from the inside.

83
00:04:50,933 --> 00:04:55,639
That's the process worth running on the next idea that lands on your desk, and the

84
00:04:55,639 --> 00:05:00,403
two-day version in the example is worth trying even if you never run the full five.

85
00:05:00,403 --> 00:05:05,109
Most ideas that eventually get shelved after real engineering time was spent would

86
00:05:05,109 --> 00:05:09,643
have failed day one or two of this sprint, cheaply, if anyone had run it first.

87
00:05:09,643 --> 00:05:14,464
That's the entire case for doing this before committing resources rather than after.

88
00:05:14,464 --> 00:05:18,539
Notice, too, that a sprint's real output isn't the yes or no by itself.

89
00:05:18,539 --> 00:05:23,015
It's the written reasoning behind it, the same discipline 7.4 asked of a model

90
00:05:23,015 --> 00:05:27,721
choice: not just the decision, but the specific answer to each day's question that

91
00:05:27,721 --> 00:05:32,485
produced it, so someone revisiting the idea in six months isn't starting from zero.

92
00:05:33,035 --> 00:05:34,668
One thing before you move on.

93
00:05:34,668 --> 00:05:39,344
Pick a real idea, not a hypothetical one, and run the first two questions honestly.

94
00:05:39,344 --> 00:05:43,738
Most people find this takes under an hour once the tools are already familiar.

95
00:05:43,738 --> 00:05:48,414
Then, whatever the sprint concludes, report it with the same confidence either way.

96
00:05:48,414 --> 00:05:52,076
A well-run no deserves the same visible credit as a well-run yes.

97
00:05:52,076 --> 00:05:55,907
Neither of these requires scheduling a formal five-day sprint today.

98
00:05:55,907 --> 00:06:00,470
The two-day version is the whole point: fast enough to run this week, on the idea

99
00:06:00,470 --> 00:06:02,554
that's already sitting in your inbox.

100
00:06:02,554 --> 00:06:07,118
Next lecture is managing stakeholder expectations, the skill that decides whether

101
00:06:07,118 --> 00:06:10,554
people actually trust the sprint's answer once you've got it.
